Modification notice (GPLv3 section 5(a))
----------------------------------------
Modified on 2026-11-04 by the RustCuiTool fork contributors.

This distribution adds, among other things:

- Rebranding to RustCuiTool and a single-file Windows build (RCT.exe).
- Multi-page (screen) support: named pages, page assignment for root
  elements, page switching in the editor, page-aware rendering and
  persistence, and migration of older project files.
- Declarative button interactions (show / hide / toggle / closeSelf /
  destroy / command) that compile to CUI `command` / `close` fields.
- A live CUI preview simulator with a routed command log.
- Multi-page C# plugin generation: one AddPage<Name> method per page and a
  command router (page / hide / toggle / close / destroy).
- Per-page JSON export, additional validation checks, the "Tabbed pages"
  template, and unit tests.
- Russian localization with Russian as the default interface language and a
  Рус / Eng switcher (the choice is stored locally).
- Multi-selection with Ctrl+click in the element tree and on the canvas,
  moving several elements together, and paste with a cascade offset.
- Layout groups (Shift+A): equal spacing/padding, automatic reflow, shared
  child size, and paste inside a group using the configured spacing.
- Alt-based distance measurements to nearby elements and canvas edges,
  Alt-drag symmetric resizing, a visible “?” shortcut button, and
  Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+X undo and redo shortcuts.
- An item-icon folder setting on the start screen with native path support
  and a browser folder picker. Image components with an item ID render the
  real Rust item icon instead of a placeholder.
- A visual item picker for the inspector: search by display name, shortname
  or item ID, category filters, a grouped grid of items with thumbnails,
  lazy thumbnail loading, and an embedded Rust item catalog.
- A single self-contained Windows build: the Rust + Tauri 2 host embeds the
  static interface into one binary (RCT.exe), so no Node.js, .NET runtime or
  side folders are needed — only the system WebView2 runtime is used.
- A local MCP server for the editor: RCT-MCP.exe ships with the desktop
  build and `npm run mcp` covers the browser build. It exposes the open
  editor to Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor, Kilo Code and other MCP
  clients over a local WebSocket, with ready-made client configurations in
  the app (AI → MCP bridge → Local).
- Custom canvas backgrounds: upload or drop a screenshot to use it as a
  background reference, replace or reset it, kept per tab and restored with
  the project.

